"A filesystem tree diagram explains directory entries and path organization while marking links or mounts that depart from a simple tree.\n\nUse when: Document an application’s file layout or storage boundary. Explain path resolution and ownership of generated files.\n\nAvoid when: The question concerns source imports rather than directory containment.\n\nVisual grammar: Parent-child edges encode directory-entry containment. File and directory markers use a small consistent legend. Symlink and mount annotations distinguish references from ordinary nested entries.\n\nBuild: Choose a root path and a bounded inclusion rule. List relevant directories and representative files. Mark generated outputs, links, and mount points explicitly. Indent or branch consistently with ample space between long path labels.\n\nCheck: Containment does not imply module dependency. Symlink targets are distinguished from copied contents. Sensitive paths and real personal data are omitted from generic examples. Branch lines and indentation guides never cross filenames.\n\nPitfalls: Presenting an aliased path as another independent copy of the same files. Expanding every generated file until the structural purpose disappears.\n\nExample: An application root contains app, catalog, and tests; a runtime-data mount is explicitly shown outside project-owned content.\n\nAlternatives: Module dependency graph, Storage partition diagram."
